refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.description;
stringfalsestream_get_contents resourcestream intnulllength&null; intoffset-1
Similar to file_get_contents, except that stream_get_contents operates on an already open stream resource and starts at its current position unless offset is given. It returns the contents in a string, up to length bytes.

&reftitle.changelog;





    &Version;
    &Description;





    8.0.0

     Now throws a ValueError if length is less than -1.
     Previously, an E_WARNING was raised and &false; was returned.




    8.0.0

     length is now nullable.

  &reftitle.examples;

   stream_get_contents example


<?php

$stream = fopen('php://memory', 'w+');
fwrite($stream, '0123456789');

// print all the data starting at the offset 3
echo stream_get_contents($stream, -1, 3), "\n";

// print the first 5 bytes
echo stream_get_contents($stream, 5, 0), "\n";

fclose($stream);


   &example.outputs;


3456789
01234

&reftitle.notes;
&note.bin-safe;


  When specifying a length value between
  1 and 32767, this function will
  immediately allocate an internal buffer of that size even if the
  actual contents are significantly shorter. Larger values do not.
